.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_destroy \- destroy a ccol_event_loop and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_destroy(loop)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro blocks until every in-flight resolved use of the handle
stops, then shuts every reactor thread down (if something did not
already shut them down) and frees every registration that remains.
Finally it sets loop to CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID.

The shutdown is the same graceful drain as ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3):
the callbacks that the loop already collected run, and loop stays a
live handle for them until the drain ends. A callback can therefore
call ccol_event_loop_remove(3), ccol_event_loop_pause(3),
ccol_event_loop_resume(3), ccol_event_loop_modify(3) and
ccol_event_loop_add(3) on loop during the destroy, with the same results
as during ccol_event_loop_shutdown(3). This makes it safe to close a
connection from its own callback (remove every registration of it,
close the fd, free arg) while another thread destroys the loop: once
the removals return, no callback of those registrations runs again.
Once the drain ends, loop stops resolving, and every later call on it
fails as for a destroyed handle.

loop must be a handle that is live at the time of the call, or
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, which does nothing. A call on a stale handle
(one that an earlier call already destroyed, or that another thread
destroys at the same time) is a FATAL ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT), not a
silent double free. The handle carries a generation tag that the
library checks before it touches the loop, so it always finds such a
value and never dereferences it.

A call on loop from inside a callback that runs on one of the reactor
threads of loop, or on one of its dispatch threads, is also a FATAL
ERROR (abort()/SIGABRT), not a deadlock or a silent no-op. Give the
destroy to another thread instead, or do it after the callback
returns.

A queue-backed registration comes from ccol_selectable_from_circq(),
ccol_selectable_from_dynq() or ccol_selectable_from_chan(). This
macro unlinks such a registration from the waiter list of its own
queue as it frees it, so a queue that outlives this ccol_event_loop is
never left with a waiter pointer that dangles. The reverse order is
the responsibility of the caller: do not destroy a queue while a
registration for it is live. ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3),
ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy(3) and ccol_channel_destroy(3) find that
case and assert against it.

Each registration that remains has a handlers.on_removed field (see
ccol_event_loop_add(3)). If it is set, it fires exactly one time as
part of the free here, which is the same guarantee that an ordinary
ccol_event_loop_remove() call gives to a live registration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  An lvalue of type ccol_event_loop.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The destroy never closes a registered fd and never destroys a
registered queue or ccol_channel; the owner of each one keeps it, as
ccol_event_loop_remove(3) leaves it.

The macro evaluates loop exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_shutdown (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
